---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Driveway Crack Fillers", url: "/best-driveway-crack-filler/" },
    { title: "How to Fill Large Cracks in Asphalt", url: "/how-to-fill-large-cracks-in-asphalt-driveway/" },
    { title: "Can You Seal a Cracked Asphalt Driveway?", url: "/can-you-seal-a-cracked-asphalt-driveway/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "Should You Fill Driveway Cracks Before Sealing?";
const description =
  "Yes, and the cure time matters more than the filling. How long crack filler needs before sealer goes over it, and which cracks mean stop.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Filling Cracks Before Sealing",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/fill-driveway-cracks-before-sealing/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to fill cracks before sealing my driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, for anything wider than a hairline. Sealer is roughly the thickness of a coat of paint and bridges nothing, so it runs into an open crack and leaves it exactly where it was. Cracks under about 1/8 inch are the exception, since sealer generally covers those.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long after filling cracks can you seal a driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Commonly 24 to 48 hours for pourable and rubberised fillers, longer for deep or layered fills, and often 30 days for cold patch. Treat those as ranges and follow the product data sheet, because cool weather, humidity and fill depth all extend them.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if you seal over uncured crack filler?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Solvent gets trapped underneath and the sealer above stays soft, wrinkles or lifts in patches. It is one of the most common causes of a sealing job failing on a driveway that was otherwise well prepared, and the only fix is stripping the affected area and starting again.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will filled cracks still show after sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Filler has a different texture and sheen, and sealer is thin enough to follow the contour rather than hide it. Striking fills flush, sanding proud ones, and applying two coats all reduce it, but a heavily repaired driveway reads as repaired rather than new.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can driveway sealer fill small cracks on its own?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only hairlines under roughly 1/8 inch. Anything wider takes the sealer down into the crack instead of across it, which wastes product and leaves the crack open. A filled 1/4 inch crack costs very little and is the part of the job that actually protects the driveway.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I fill cracks before or after pressure washing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "After. Wash and clean the driveway first so the cracks are clear of debris and vegetation, then let everything dry completely, then fill. A damp crack prevents the filler from adhering, so the drying step between washing and filling is not optional.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What if my driveway has alligator cracking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "That interconnected pattern means the base beneath has failed, and crack filler does nothing structural for it. The area needs patching or resurfacing. Sealing over it hides the problem for a season and it returns worse, having consumed the budget the real repair needed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need backer rod for deep cracks?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It helps a great deal. Foam rod pushed into a deep crack fills the void so the filler only caps the top, which uses far less product, cures far more reliably, and prevents the endless sinking and topping up that deep fills otherwise require.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Driveway & Concrete",
    tags: [
      "fill driveway cracks before sealing",
      "crack filler before sealer",
      "driveway sealing preparation",
      "driveway & concrete",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Driveway Crack Fillers",
    link: "/best-driveway-crack-filler/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Straight Answer
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Should You Fill Cracks <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Before Sealing?</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Sealer is a thin liquid that bridges nothing. Poured over an open crack it runs straight in, disappears, and leaves the crack exactly where it was, only now hidden under a fresh black finish.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#why-first"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Sequence
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="13.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Filling Comes First</a>
          <a href="#cure-time" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Cure Time</a>
          <a href="#the-order" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Order of Operations</a>
          <a href="#when-not-to" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. When Not To</a>
          <a href="#appearance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Appearance</a>
          <a href="#products" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Matching Filler</a>
          <a href="#timing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Seasonal Timing</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, always, and in that order. The question worth asking is not whether to fill first but how long to wait afterwards, because sealing over filler that has not cured is how a properly prepared driveway still fails.
        </p>

        <div id="why-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Filling Has to Come First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Driveway sealer is a surface treatment roughly the thickness of a coat of paint. It protects asphalt from ultraviolet light, oxidation and water, and it does that by coating the surface rather than by filling anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Poured over an open crack, it behaves exactly as a thin liquid should: it runs in, sinks below the surface, and leaves a depression that reappears within days. You have used sealer at crack-filler prices to accomplish nothing, and the crack continues to take on water underneath a finish that looks fine.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Crack Width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Then Seal?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hairline, under 1/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealer usually bridges it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, no filling needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/8 to 1/2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pourable or bottled crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, after cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/2 to 1 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rubberised filler, often in layers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, after cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 1 in, or deep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cold patch or hot patch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, after cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Alligator cracking over an area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patch or resurface, not filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only after repair</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cracks with movement or heaving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Investigate the base first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No, fix the cause</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Real Reason It Matters</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water in a crack is what destroys a driveway, not sunlight.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Sealer slows oxidation, which is a slow cosmetic decline. Water entering a crack, reaching the base, and freezing is what breaks a driveway apart, and it works fast. Filling cracks is therefore the part of the job that protects the structure, and sealing is the part that protects the appearance. Doing the second without the first is the wrong way round.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cure-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Long to Wait Before Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the step people get wrong, because crack filler skins over quickly and feels finished long before it is. Sealing over uncured filler traps solvent underneath, and the sealer above it stays soft, wrinkles, or lifts in patches.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Filler Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Touch Dry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Before Sealing Over</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pourable cold crack filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 24-48 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rubberised emulsion filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commonly 24-48 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deep or layered fills</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Allow extra, often several days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold patch for large areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Immediate, compacted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 30 days before sealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hot-applied crack sealant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rapid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Follow the product sheet</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat those as ranges rather than rules, because the product data sheet is the authority and the figures vary widely between brands. Cool weather, high humidity, deep fills and thick applications all extend them, sometimes considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold patch is the one that surprises people. It is a compacted asphalt repair rather than a liquid, and it needs to be driven on and allowed to settle before anything coats it, which is why thirty days is a common instruction. Sealing fresh cold patch usually produces a soft patch that stays soft. Our guide to <a href="/cold-patch-asphalt/">cold patch asphalt</a> covers the material properly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Tell It Is Actually Ready</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Press a thumbnail into the filler. It should be firm rather than leaving a lasting impression.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Look for any remaining solvent smell, which means the fill is still off-gassing.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check that the surface has gone from glossy to matte, which usually indicates the skin has cured through.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Test the deepest fill rather than the shallowest, because it is the one still curing.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If in doubt, wait another day. Nothing is lost by waiting and a great deal is lost by not.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="the-order" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Full Order of Operations
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crack filling sits in the middle of a sequence, and getting the surrounding steps right is what makes the filling hold.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Clear the cracks first. Pull weeds out by the root, and blow or scrape loose debris out of the channel.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Kill any vegetation properly rather than cutting it off at the surface, because roots continue to widen the crack.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Clean the whole driveway, degreasing any oil spots, since sealer will not bond over oil.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Let the surface dry completely. A damp crack means the filler will not adhere.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Fill the cracks, working from the widest down, and slightly overfill to allow for settling.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Let the filler cure fully, per the product sheet rather than per the clock.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Seal the driveway, once the weather window is right.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Two of those steps are the ones most often skipped. Weeds cut off at the surface regrow through both the filler and the sealer within a season, and oil left in place is a spot where nothing bonds, so the sealer lifts there first. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/">cleaning an asphalt driveway before sealing</a> and <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains</a> cover both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Weather Compounds</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You now need two dry windows, not one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Crack filler needs its cure time and sealer needs its own dry window before and after application. That means planning across several consecutive dry days rather than a single free afternoon, and it is the main reason people seal over uncured filler: the forecast turned and they went ahead anyway. Our guide to the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a> covers the weather requirements.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. When Filling and Sealing Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some damage is not a crack problem, and filling it postpones a repair that is only going to get more expensive.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Do</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Alligator cracking over an area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Base failure beneath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patch or resurface the area</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A crack that reopens every year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Movement below the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Investigate the base</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Depressions holding water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Base settlement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Repair before any coating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crumbling, loose aggregate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Surface has failed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Resurfacing territory</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Potholes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full-depth failure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patch properly, then wait to seal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Heaving in one area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roots or frost below</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Find the cause first</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Alligator cracking is the important row. The interconnected pattern that looks like reptile skin means the base beneath has failed, and filler poured into it does nothing structural. Sealing over it hides it for a season and it returns worse. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">fixing a crumbling asphalt driveway</a> and <a href="/driveway-resurfacing-cost/">resurfacing costs</a> cover the alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest test is proportion. A driveway with scattered cracks is a filling and sealing job. A driveway where the cracked and failing area is a substantial share of the surface has moved past maintenance, and money spent on sealer there is money that will not be available for the resurfacing it actually needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="appearance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Filled Cracks Look Like Afterwards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Worth setting expectations, because this surprises people who were hoping sealing would make the driveway look new.
          </p>

          <p>
            Filled cracks remain visible after sealing. The filler has a different texture and often a slightly different sheen from the surrounding surface, and sealer is thin enough that it follows the contour rather than hiding it. On a driveway with many fills, the result reads as a repaired driveway rather than a fresh one.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Overfilling slightly and then striking it flush reduces the ridge that shows most.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Sanding a proud fill once cured helps it disappear into the surrounding surface.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Two coats of sealer blend fills better than one, since the second evens out the texture difference.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Filler applied wider than the crack, in a broad band, is far more visible than a neat fill.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A sand-filled or aggregate sealer hides texture differences better than a plain one.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Expect fills to show through more in the first season and to blend somewhat as the sealer weathers.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            If appearance is the actual goal rather than protection, that changes the calculation. Sealing makes a sound driveway look considerably better and it will not make a heavily cracked one look new, which is where resurfacing enters the conversation. Our guide to <a href="/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/">how many coats of sealer</a> covers what a second coat does and does not buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="products" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Matching Filler to Crack
          </h2>

          <p>
            Using the wrong filler for the width is the second most common error after skipping the cure time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pourable bottled fillers are made for narrow cracks and self-level into them. Used in a wide or deep crack they slump, sink and need repeated topping up over weeks. Trowel-grade and rubberised products hold their shape in wider gaps but are wasted on hairlines they cannot penetrate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product Style</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fails When</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pourable bottle filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Narrow cracks up to about 1/2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Used in wide or deep cracks; it sinks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rubberised trowel filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wider cracks, holds shape</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Used on hairlines it cannot enter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foam backer rod plus filler</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Deep cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Omitted, and the fill sinks endlessly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold patch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Potholes and large voids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Used on narrow cracks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealer alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hairlines under 1/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Used on anything wider</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Backer rod is the trick most people have not heard of. In a deep crack, foam rod pushed down first fills the void so the filler only has to cap the top rather than fill the whole depth. It saves product, cures far more reliably, and stops the endless topping up that deep fills otherwise need. Our roundups of the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> and <a href="/best-asphalt-crack-filler/">best asphalt crack fillers</a> cover the products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fitting It Into the Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because you need two cure windows, timing matters more than for sealing alone.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Aim for a run of several consecutive dry days with temperatures comfortably above the sealer minimum.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Fill cracks early in that window so the filler has its full cure before the sealer goes down.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Avoid filling late in autumn, when cool nights extend cure times well beyond the label figure.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Do not fill in the rain window, since water in the crack prevents adhesion entirely.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Remember that surface temperature can differ substantially from air temperature on a sunny day.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If the weather turns after filling, wait. Cured filler under delayed sealer is fine; the reverse is not.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Autumn is generally the strongest sealing window, with moderate temperatures and lower ultraviolet intensity, and it also gives the driveway protection going into the freeze-thaw season that does the real damage. That is doubly true when you have just filled cracks, since the whole point was keeping water out before it freezes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the temperature, humidity and rain requirements in detail, see our guide to the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a>, and for what happens if the forecast betrays you, <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Cracks and Hoping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer is about as thick as a coat of paint and bridges nothing wider than a hairline. Poured over an open crack it runs in, sinks, and leaves the crack exactly where it was under a fresh black finish. The crack keeps taking on water, which is what actually destroys a driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before the Filler Has Cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crack filler skins over quickly and feels finished long before it is. Sealing over it traps solvent underneath, and the sealer above stays soft, wrinkles or lifts. Most fillers want 24 to 48 hours, deep fills want longer, and cold patch commonly wants 30 days.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Weeds Off Instead of Killing Them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A weed trimmed at the surface still has its root in the crack, and it grows straight back through both the filler and the sealer within a season. Kill the vegetation properly and clear the root before filling, or you are sealing a driveway that will crack again on schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Pourable Filler in a Deep Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bottled self-levelling fillers are made for narrow cracks. In a deep one they slump and sink and need topping up for weeks. Push foam backer rod in first so the filler only caps the top, which cures reliably, uses far less product and actually stays put.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Alligator Cracking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The interconnected reptile-skin pattern means the base underneath has failed, and filler poured into it does nothing structural. Sealing over it buys one season and it comes back worse. That area needs patching or resurfacing, and sealer spent on it is money unavailable for the real repair.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to fill cracks before sealing my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for anything wider than a hairline. Sealer is roughly the thickness of a coat of paint and bridges nothing, so it runs into an open crack and leaves it exactly where it was. Cracks under about 1/8 inch are the exception, since sealer generally covers those.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after filling cracks can you seal a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Commonly 24 to 48 hours for pourable and rubberised fillers, longer for deep or layered fills, and often 30 days for cold patch. Treat those as ranges and follow the product data sheet, because cool weather, humidity and fill depth all extend them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you seal over uncured crack filler?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Solvent gets trapped underneath and the sealer above stays soft, wrinkles or lifts in patches. It is one of the most common causes of a sealing job failing on a driveway that was otherwise well prepared, and the only fix is stripping the affected area and starting again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will filled cracks still show after sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Filler has a different texture and sheen, and sealer is thin enough to follow the contour rather than hide it. Striking fills flush, sanding proud ones, and applying two coats all reduce it, but a heavily repaired driveway reads as repaired rather than new.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can driveway sealer fill small cracks on its own?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only hairlines under roughly 1/8 inch. Anything wider takes the sealer down into the crack instead of across it, which wastes product and leaves the crack open. A filled 1/4 inch crack costs very little and is the part of the job that actually protects the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I fill cracks before or after pressure washing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After. Wash and clean the driveway first so the cracks are clear of debris and vegetation, then let everything dry completely, then fill. A damp crack prevents the filler from adhering, so the drying step between washing and filling is not optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if my driveway has alligator cracking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That interconnected pattern means the base beneath has failed, and crack filler does nothing structural for it. The area needs patching or resurfacing. Sealing over it hides the problem for a season and it returns worse, having consumed the budget the real repair needed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need backer rod for deep cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps a great deal. Foam rod pushed into a deep crack fills the void so the filler only caps the top, which uses far less product, cures far more reliably, and prevents the endless sinking and topping up that deep fills otherwise require.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

