        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Fill first, always. Sealer is about as thick as a coat of paint, so it bridges nothing wider than a hairline and simply runs into an open crack, leaving it where it was under a fresh black finish. Filling cracks is the part of the job that protects the driveway; sealing is the part that protects how it looks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The step that catches people is the cure time. Crack filler skins over fast and feels finished long before it is, and sealing over it traps solvent so the sealer above stays soft or lifts. Plan on 24 to 48 hours for most fillers, longer for deep fills, and commonly 30 days for cold patch, following the product sheet rather than the clock.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And know when to stop. Scattered cracks are a filling and sealing job. Alligator cracking, depressions holding water, or a surface losing its aggregate mean the base has failed, and filler does nothing structural for any of them. Sealing over that hides it for one season and consumes the budget the resurfacing needed.
          </p>
        </div>


